+++
title = "Implement Orchestrator"
task = "TASK-00.02.00"
version = "0.2.0"
+++

# Objective

Implement the platform-dependent execution orchestrator for `iasi-setup-dev`.

The Java application already provides command-line argument processing, configuration management and execution context.

The orchestrator is responsible for extracting, preparing and executing the platform-specific scripts, waiting for their completion and managing their return codes.

Java orchestrates. Platform-specific scripts implement the actual setup functionality.

# Requirements

## 1. Operating system selection

Determine the operating system on which the Java application is running.

Operating system selection MUST be implemented using a `switch` statement.

Initially supported operating systems:

- Windows.
- Linux.
- macOS.

Each operating system MUST be treated independently.

Linux and macOS MUST NOT share the same implementation directory, even when both use shell scripts.

Unsupported operating systems MUST generate the corresponding corporate error code.

## 2. Resource structure

Platform-specific resources MUST be located under `resources/bin`, using a separate directory for each operating system.

```text
resources/
└── bin/
    ├── windows/
    │   ├── setup.ps1
    │   └── ...
    ├── linux/
    │   ├── setup.sh
    │   └── ...
    └── macos/
        ├── setup.sh
        └── ...
```

Each directory contains all the scripts and auxiliary resources required by the corresponding operating system.

The orchestrator MUST NOT extract resources belonging to other operating systems.

## 3. Resource extraction

Obtain the operating system temporary directory using the standard Java mechanisms.

Create an `iasi` directory within the temporary directory.

Extract the selected operating system resources into its corresponding subdirectory.

```text
<temporary-directory>/
└── iasi/
    ├── windows/
    ├── linux/
    └── macos/
```

Resource extraction MUST:

- Work when the application is executed from a packaged JAR.
- Extract the complete selected directory recursively.
- Preserve the original directory structure.
- Replace previously extracted resources to prevent execution of obsolete scripts.
- Include auxiliary resources, not only executable scripts.

Only the directory corresponding to the current operating system needs to be created and populated.

## 4. Working directory and path resolution

The directory used to extract scripts MUST NOT become the application working directory.

The orchestrator MUST preserve the original working directory from which the Java application was invoked.

The default values MUST remain:

- `--definitions`: `.`
- `--machines`: `.`

When these parameters are not explicitly configured, they MUST reference the original working directory.

Relative paths MUST retain their original meaning and MUST NOT be resolved against the temporary script directory.

The orchestrator MUST:

- Execute the entry-point script using its absolute path.
- Explicitly configure the subprocess working directory using the original working directory.
- Preserve the existing configuration precedence rules.

Platform-specific scripts MUST locate their own auxiliary scripts and resources relative to their own location, independently of the working directory.

The extraction process MUST be transparent to the user.

## 5. Execution permissions

Prepare the extracted resources before executing the entry-point script.

### 5.1. Windows

- Unblock extracted PowerShell scripts using `Unblock-File`.
- Ensure that the extracted PowerShell scripts can be executed.
- Apply the required PowerShell execution policy at process level.
- Do not permanently modify the user or machine execution policy.
- Wait for the permission preparation process to complete.
- Retrieve and process its return code.

### 5.2. Linux

- Grant execution permissions to the corresponding shell scripts.
- Verify that the permission preparation operation completes successfully.
- Retrieve and process its return code.

### 5.3. macOS

- Grant execution permissions to the corresponding shell scripts.
- Verify that the permission preparation operation completes successfully.
- Retrieve and process its return code.

## 6. Script execution

Execute the entry-point script corresponding to the selected operating system.

| Operating system | Entry point |
|---|---|
| Windows | `setup.ps1` |
| Linux | `setup.sh` |
| macOS | `setup.sh` |

Use the existing execution context to obtain the parameters required by the script.

The orchestrator MUST:

- Reuse the existing parsed arguments and configuration.
- Translate parameters into the existing script command-line interface when necessary.
- Preserve argument boundaries.
- Correctly handle paths and parameter values containing spaces.
- Use the absolute path of the extracted entry-point script.
- Preserve the original working directory.
- Wait synchronously until the subprocess completes.
- Retrieve the subprocess exit code.

Standard output and standard error MUST be handled without causing subprocess blocking.

The orchestrator MUST NOT duplicate the functionality already implemented by the platform-specific scripts.

## 7. Return code management

All return codes MUST follow the existing IASI corporate return code specification.

Return codes generated by the orchestrator and the executed processes MUST be accumulated using the bitwise OR operator.

```java
rc |= processRC;
```

This includes:

- Operating system selection.
- Resource extraction.
- Permission preparation.
- Process creation.
- Script execution.
- Process completion.
- Internal orchestration errors.

Return codes MUST NOT be accumulated using arithmetic addition or numerical maximum.

An operation that produces an error preventing subsequent execution MUST stop the affected execution flow.

The accumulated return code MUST be preserved and returned to the caller.

The orchestrator MUST NOT invoke `System.exit()`.

Only the application entry point is responsible for terminating the Java process.

```java
System.exit(rc);
```

## 8. Execution flow

The logical execution sequence MUST be:

1. Obtain the existing execution context.
2. Preserve the original working directory.
3. Identify the operating system.
4. Select the platform using `switch`.
5. Prepare the temporary destination directory.
6. Extract the platform-specific resources.
7. Prepare execution permissions.
8. Build the platform-specific command.
9. Execute the entry-point script.
10. Wait for process completion.
11. Retrieve and accumulate the return codes.
12. Return the accumulated return code.

## 9. Corporate development requirements

- Follow the existing IASI structured programming guidelines.
- Follow the corporate Java naming conventions.
- Follow the corporate Javadoc specification.
- Reuse the existing execution context.
- Reuse the existing return code definitions.
- Preserve the existing configuration precedence.
- Maintain a clear separation of responsibilities.
- Do not duplicate argument parsing.
- Do not refactor unrelated packages.
- Do not introduce unnecessary dependencies.
- Do not implement functionality outside the scope of this task.

## 10. Validation

Validation MUST be restricted to the scope of this task.

Verify:

- Successful compilation.
- Resource extraction from the packaged JAR.
- Independent operating system resource directories.
- Execution permission preparation.
- Correct argument propagation.
- Preservation of the original working directory.
- Default values for `--definitions` and `--machines`.
- Synchronous subprocess execution.
- Return code accumulation using bitwise OR.

Execute only tests directly related to the implemented functionality.

Do not execute the complete test suite, unrelated integration tests or the complete corporate Quality Gate.

Report any platform-specific validation that could not be performed in the current environment.

## 11. Version

Update the application version to `0.2.0`.

## 12. Acceptance criteria

The Java application successfully delegates execution to the corresponding platform-specific setup script, preserving the existing configuration, arguments, working directory and corporate return code semantics.

The temporary location of the extracted resources MUST NOT alter the functional behaviour of the application.